<AtlasLayout title="Methodology">
  <article class="page-width content-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">How to read the atlas</p><h1>Mechanisms with context.</h1><p class="hero-description">A relationship is useful only when its meaning, evidence, and limits are clear. The atlas keeps those details close to the map.</p>
    <aside class="preview-notice"><span class="notice-symbol" aria-hidden="true">i</span><div><strong>Milestone 1 development preview</strong><p>The current map is an illustrative fixture. It has no domain-review approval and must not be used as a scientific reference or for medical decisions.</p></div></aside>
    <h2>Read a connection</h2><p>Arrows show directed relationships. A line without arrowheads represents a symmetric association, which does not assert a causal direction. Select a node or line to see the attached claim and its evidence. A feedback loop is shown by distinct connections in both directions.</p>
    <h2>Inspect the evidence</h2><p>Claims carry a disease context, linked evidence items, sources, and limitations. Supporting, qualifying, and contradicting evidence have distinct roles. An empty category means no evidence has been entered in that category; it does not establish that such evidence is absent from the literature.</p>
    <h2 id="scoring">Scores: Not assessed</h2><p>The fixture has no numerical assessments. Every mechanism uses a common baseline size and neutral evidence styling. Position does not imply importance, and zero is never substituted for an unassessed value. Calibrated and approved scoring may be added after the MVP.</p>
    <h2 id="illustrative-fixture">About the illustrative fixture</h2><p>The five-entity, six-relationship sample exists to test navigation, graph rendering, selection, a feedback loop, and symmetric association. Its biological labels are illustrative. Every scientific record is a candidate and every claim is unverified.</p><p>The linked “source” for this fixture is this documentation page. It is a deliberate interface placeholder, not a publication citation. The curated SLE milestone must replace fixture claims with directly relevant primary-source evidence before scientific review.</p>
    <h2 id="publication-policy">Scientific review and publication</h2><p>A curated release needs an explicitly configured domain reviewer and approval of the exact candidate revision and content digest. Changes after approval invalidate that decision. Candidate and under-review scientific records cannot be published.</p><p>Published releases will embed their entities, relationships, claims, evidence, assessments, sources, contexts, and layout. The first release may have a null parent. Later releases must reference an existing immutable parent. A working-record edit cannot change an earlier release.</p><p>The development build is an internal preview, not the scientific publication step. Publication validation must reject this illustrative candidate. The repository review policy explains the configured reviewer and approval requirements.</p>
    <h2>Scientific use</h2><p>Causome is intended for mechanism exploration by scientifically literate users. The initial atlas does not provide patient-specific advice, diagnosis, or treatment recommendations. Source verification and human domain review are required before the MVP launch.</p>
